e diagonal open-3 at one end. If White doesn’t block I10, Black plays I10 next for open-4.  \n2. **I10** – same idea, other end.  \n3. **G7** – connects F7 horizontally and makes vertical G7–G8–G9 (but G10 is White, so vertical is blocked at bottom; still, horizontal potential with F7–G7–H8–? later).  \n4. **H10** – vertical with H8–H9, but G10 blocks vertical extension downward; not as strong.  \n\nBest is **E6** or **I10**.  \nWhich is better?  \n\n- **E6** leaves I10 open; White might block at I10 next, but then Black still has other threats.  \n- **I10** is also empty and part of the open-3, but White’s G10 is nearby — actually I10 is far from G10, no interference.  \n\nActually, check: If Black plays E6, the diagonal becomes E6–F7–G8–H9 (4 stones), but is it open at both ends? After E6, the diagonal’s other end is I10 (empty) and beyond E6 is D5 (empty), so that’s actually an **open-4**? Wait, count: E6 (new), F7 (X), G8 (X), H9 (X) → that’s 4 in a row. Both ends D5 and I10 empty. Yes, that’s an open-4! That means White must block either D5 or I10 next move or Black wins.  \n\nBut open-4 means if White blocks one end, Black plays the other end and gets 5. So **E6 is actually a winning move** because it creates an open-4 in the diagonal.  \n\nLet’s verify:  \nDiagonal from D5 to I10:  \nD5 (empty)  \nE6 (X if played)  \nF7 (X)  \nG8 (X)  \nH9 (X)  \nI10 (empty)  \n\nThat’s 4 in a row with both ends open → open-4 → White cannot stop both D5 and I10 in one move → Black wins next turn.  \n\nYes, E6 is the winning move.  \n\n---\n\n**Final check:** E6 is not in the occupied list, so it’s legal.  \n\n---\n\n**Evaluation:** E6 creates an open-4 diagonal, which is an unstoppable threat.  \n\n**Reason:** After E6, White can only block one of D5 or I10, and Black plays the other to make 5 in a row and win.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n```json\n{"col": "E", "row": 6}\n```'}
